{"author":{"id":"ce771b8a9b8c30a8aac165a8b1cdb0957637d3941276ec4f27335daee16d79de"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794832776,"nonce":"zBeQtvi2zPHifQcmpPtBe5JGqNY=","title":"Leaflog's grow-science numbers — the preset ranges growers plan around and the","message":"engine constants that drive verdicts, forecasts and advice — do not say where\nthey come from. Some carry a free-text note (\"contested — one-source\"),\nstrains carry a single breeder URL for the whole record, and the engine\nconstants (vapour-pressure-deficit bands, the lamp-height wattage table,\ntemperature shifts, the EC-step clamp, the forecast's default phase windows)\nare bare numbers in code. A grower who asks \"why does the app say my humidity\nshould be 55%?\" has nowhere to look, and the project cannot tell which numbers\nrest on controlled research versus folklore. The seed-data discipline in the\nagent instructions already demands a cited source for every default number,\nre-checked against at least two sources — that check has never actually been\nrun against the peer-reviewed literature.\n\n\nEvery grow-science value carries an **evidence tier** — one of eight canonical\nlabels from `peer-reviewed-cannabis` down to `unverified` — and references\nentries in a **citation registry**: a machine-readable list of studies and\nsources (authors, year, title, venue, DOI or URL, tier, access, checked date,\ncultivar) kept as a shared asset in leaflog-core. A value's tier is its\nhighest-tier source, and its stored range must cover every source's value.\nControlled research on hemp or another crop may ground a value as\n`peer-reviewed-transferred` with a conservatively widened range; a grower\nsees a tier badge and the source list wherever presets appear. Engine\nconstants become sourced preset data so the same provenance rule covers them.\nThe existing numbers are then audited topic by topic against the literature\n(VPD, lamp height, feed, forecast windows, seedbank/strain labels), and three\nliterature-backed parameter families are added: per-phase DLI/PPFD targets, a\nroot-zone temperature band, and a photoperiod critical-day-length preset.\nDecisions are recorded in the decision log as D074 and D075; the literature\nsurvey that backs the tiers lives in the local scratch survey.\n\n\n1. As a grower, I want every preset number to show where it came from, so that I can decide how much to trust it.\n2. As a grower, I want an evidence tier on each value, so that peer-reviewed numbers and folklore look different at a glance.\n3. As a grower, I want to see the study behind a tier (who, when, where published), so that I can read the source myself.\n4. As a grower, I want values grounded in hemp or other-crop research marked as transferred, so that I am never misled about drug-type-cannabis evidence.\n5. As a grower, I want ranges that visibly span disagreeing sources, so that disagreement is honest rather than hidden.\n6. As a grower, I want strain provenance to stay exactly as usable as today, so that structured citations do not change what I see.\n7. As a grower, I want the lamp advisor to explain its height bands, so that \"330 W → 40–50 cm\" is not an article of faith.\n8. As a grower, I want VPD bands backed by controlled studies, so that humidity verdicts mean something.\n9. As a grower, I want DLI/PPFD targets per phase, so that I can set my light distance and dimmer with a target in mind.\n10. As a grower, I want those light targets to come from dose-response research, so that they are not grower myth.\n11. As a grower, I want a root-zone temperature band, so that I know whether my medium is part of a problem.\n12. As a grower, I want the photoperiod threshold grounded, so that flipping at 12 hours is a choice, not an assumption.\n13. As a grower, I want a day/night-difference tip in the phase coach, so that stretch advice reflects controlled-environment research.\n14. As a grower, I want parameters I do not use (CO₂ tanks, UV fixtures) left alone, so that the app does not grow settings nobody touches.\n15. As a grower, I want seed-viability windows to admit when no controlled studies exist, so that the seedbank is honest about its limits.\n16. As a grower editing a clone, I want user edits to keep winning, so that provenance plumbing never overwrites my values.\n17. As the maintainer, I want one registry entry per study, so that a correction lands in one place and every referencing value updates.\n18. As the maintainer, I want registry records to carry a checked date and access status, so that the next audit knows what was verified, when, and how.\n19. As the maintainer, I want registry records to carry the study's cultivar, so that single-cultivar ranges stay visibly narrow.\n20. As the maintainer, I want a rendered sources document, so that the citation story is readable without the app.\n21. As the maintainer, I want legacy free-text provenance removed once migration completes, so that there is one representation, not two.\n22. As an implementing agent, I want the registry to refuse unknown citation ids, so that a typo cannot ship as silent provenance loss.\n23. As an implementing agent, I want golden tests to pin engine numbers through the audit, so that retiering changes labels and ranges without silently changing verdicts.\n24. As a grower on a fresh install, I want upgraded databases to keep their bundled presets and provenance, so that the insert-new-only upgrade rule holds.\n\n\n- Evidence model per D074: eight canonical tier labels, strongest to weakest —\n  `peer-reviewed-cannabis`, `peer-reviewed-transferred`,\n  `peer-reviewed-review`, `extension`, `breeder`, `grower-reported`,\n  `inferred`, `unverified`. The strain evidence tags keep their names and map\n  onto the same vocabulary.\n- Combination rule: a value's tier is its highest-tier source, and its range\n  must cover every source's value. A grower report widens a study-backed\n  range but never raises its tier.\n- Transfer policy: hemp or other-crop controlled research grounds values as\n  `peer-reviewed-transferred` with conservatively widened ranges; absence of\n  cannabis-specific work is recorded, not papered over.\n- Citation registry: a TOML asset in leaflog-core, one record per source —\n  id, authors, year, title, venue, DOI or URL, tier, access, checked date,\n  cultivar. Preset values reference registry ids. The loader refuses unknown\n  ids. A rendered sources document is generated from it. The per-strain\n  breeder URL and licence note migrate into the registry (a breeder page is a\n  `breeder`-tier registry record).\n- Engine constants (VPD phase bands, lamp-height wattage table,\n  temperature-threshold shifts, EC-step clamp, forecast default phase\n  windows) move from code into the sourced preset layer. Physics stays in\n  code: the saturation-vapour-pressure formula keeps a doc-comment citation,\n  not a registry row.\n- Tracer order: substrate presets first (registry end to end), then strain\n  provenance, then engine constants. Free-text provenance is kept beside the\n  new form until the closing ticket removes it (expand–contract).\n- Audit sequence per D075: VPD bands, lamp-height table, feed-fit and\n  substrate bands, forecast windows, seedbank and strain labels. Each audit\n  lands its sources in the registry, retiers honestly (including\n  `unverified` where the literature has nothing), and adjusts ranges only\n  under the cover-all rule.\n- Extend phase per D075: per-phase DLI/PPFD target bands (dose-response\n  studies), root-zone temperature band, photoperiod critical-day-length\n  preset (~12.5–13.25 h) with the forecast flip audited against it; a\n  day/night-difference coach tip. Deferred: CO₂ enrichment, spectrum/UV\n  guidance, ammonium/nitrate ratio.\n- UI stays minimal: tier badge plus source list on preset surfaces. No\n  evidence browser; the prose gate applies to anything fancier.\n- Upgrade rule unchanged: bundled provenance data is seeded insert-new-only;\n  user edits and clones never touched; corrections ship as new registry\n  revisions.\n- Registry records carry a cultivar field because many cannabis studies are\n  single-cultivar.\n\n\n- Test external behaviour, not implementation details. Three existing seams,\n  no new ones: the backend HTTP API integration suite (provenance fields on\n  preset responses), the core pure-engine golden/property suites (pinned\n  numbers through every audit), and the browser end-to-end suite (tier badge\n  and source list actually render).\n- The registry loader is an internal detail: its refusal of unknown ids is\n  observable through the API suite and a focused unit test on the loader\n  boundary only.\n- The combination rule (highest tier wins, range covers all sources) is a\n  pure function in core with table-driven unit tests.\n- Prior art: the API integration tests that drive a seeded store, the\n  forecast golden tests, the VPD golden tests, and the end-to-end form\n  cases under the simulated ingress prefix.\n- Each audit ticket updates goldens only where a range legitimately moves;\n  retiering alone must leave every verdict unchanged.\n\n\n- CO₂ enrichment setpoints, light-spectrum/UV guidance, and ammonium/nitrate\n  ratio advice (deferred with reasons in the decision log).\n- An in-app evidence browser, tooltips linking to papers, or coach prose\n  beyond the tier badge and source list.\n- New controlled-literature claims for germination, drying and curing\n  windows (none exists; they are honestly retiered, not invented).\n- Multi-cultivar range modelling beyond the cultivar field.\n- Any change to strain CRUD, cloning, or bundled-data immutability rules.\n\n\n- The decision-log entries backing this spec (D074, D075) and the new\n  glossary terms are being committed alongside this effort; the survey with\n  full source ids, candidate ranges and access notes lives in the local\n  scratch directory as working notes.\n- Many source studies are single-cultivar and chamber-grown; the cultivar\n  field and conservative range widening are the standing mitigations.\n- Tracker sync: bug objects are pushed explicitly, separate from file history.","files":null}]}